{:edb/db-before-t 2321 :edb/db-after-t 2322 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535634 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:08:11.848Z" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124238 1100 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 2x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n1. $A$ is not invertible\n2. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n3. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124238 1100 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/ac61fea55da2b0f5b4c2b21b8915c7eb7fc30c576b2ca7dea830ae0aa9183dd6.png)\n\n- $A$ is not invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124238 1145 "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nCol(A) = R^{4}; dim(Col(A)) = 4 = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe conclude that\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0$.\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124238 1145 "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/f57560debe62c432de44b86e2b1f569b703b9ef3faae46bcc2df18cca17a2c62.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/65f646a200575b7299fc0278f7a5dc73cba98e293943bdb592ca24c4dd01df0f.png)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124351 1100 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\n- $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124351 1100 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\text{__________}\\,$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n3. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124351 1145 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/7ab0bb5d64ace5e31ea02d022aa0d8696deb3b62555cfccac069ba74ff750526.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124351 1145 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $A$ is invertible $Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\det(A) = 0$.* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$Since $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0$.\n- The following statement is true:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$Indeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n$.\n- The following statement is true:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ Indeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n$, which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124352 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/0ab76d251e530205b2fad22f1b4a4677370459e5cd7e19da4220c7c4baac51a3.png)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124352 1100 "$Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; W W W = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124352 1145 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following:\n\n> *Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n> $Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null (A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$." 13194139535634 false] [17592186124352 1145 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068722/assets/c9e4725a19de7c9a07cec4b929905c3d1907871b46deb3ab197b7481aa2a0467.png)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358528 13194139535634 true] [17592186124432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358535 13194139535634 true] [17592186124432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358569 13194139535634 true] [17592186124432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358576 13194139535634 true] [17592186124432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358597 13194139535634 true] [17592186124550 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358583 13194139535634 true] [17592186124550 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358590 13194139535634 true] [17592186124550 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358604 13194139535634 true] [17592186124550 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358611 13194139535634 true] [17592186124551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358521 13194139535634 true] [17592186124551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358548 13194139535634 true] [17592186124551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358555 13194139535634 true] [17592186124551 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358562 13194139535634 true] [17592186124709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186121273 13194139535634 false] [17592186124709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358542 13194139535634 true] [17592186124709 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535634 true] [17592186124709 1100 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & =n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139535634 true] [17592186124709 1100 "$Ais invertible⇔{Col(A) = R^{n}; W W = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\cdot n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139535634 false] [17592186124709 1145 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{dim(Col}(A)).$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186125084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535634 true] [17592186125084 1100 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}$\n- $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" 13194139535634 true] [17592186125084 1100 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $7 \\cdot 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible, then $__________$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $det(A) = 0$\n2. $Col(A) = R^{7}$\n3. $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" 13194139535634 false] [17592186125084 1145 "If $A$ is an $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358522 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1089 "q-141192" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1100 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))==\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1113 #uuid "622f3852-73e0-573b-9e15-ed3777600f27" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358521 1145 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358523 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358524 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358525 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358526 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358527 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1041 "selection" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358525 13194139535634 true] [17592186358522 1110 #uuid "d4427eeb-5ce3-5c7e-83a1-2a01e296cb99" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358523 1034 "{1}" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535634 true] [17592186358523 1111 #uuid "fa054469-f6b7-5561-a3c6-4ddb18025ac5" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358524 1034 "{n}" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535634 true] [17592186358524 1111 #uuid "efed5d7f-4356-5220-978e-e809c4d45513" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358525 1034 "n" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535634 true] [17592186358525 1111 #uuid "365378ec-3223-5905-9912-318f8563dfc0" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358526 1034 "1" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535634 true] [17592186358526 1111 #uuid "d5113854-0df7-5699-972c-3780ead3afc7" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358527 1034 "0" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535634 true] [17592186358527 1111 #uuid "b44c5db6-1c99-5d70-82ff-9bfcf7cb44d4" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358529 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1089 "q-141199" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1100 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1113 #uuid "7c701e87-53a1-5406-ab33-1b75f3ea593b" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358528 1145 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358530 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358531 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358532 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358533 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358534 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1041 "selection" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358530 13194139535634 true] [17592186358529 1110 #uuid "7082c85f-a381-57a4-a45b-30ec8ba228b8" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358530 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358530 1111 #uuid "123fc04e-4909-5609-aee0-32b5f60b795e" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358531 1034 "I only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358531 1111 #uuid "947919d2-63e1-5774-b9bd-af379aeb50e2" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358532 1034 "III only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358532 1111 #uuid "75e32c15-af80-5d43-b41a-6e3c9b3a99c8" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358533 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358533 1111 #uuid "e2ef2978-6469-5b50-bb76-9cfa29f42b1a" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358534 1034 "II only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358534 1111 #uuid "cb69d318-2175-5a82-a86a-deca237f31d4" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358536 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1089 "q-141200" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1100 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1113 #uuid "95fb61ee-6372-5926-b11d-d18f6bad7442" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358535 1145 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358537 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358538 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358539 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358540 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358541 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1041 "selection" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358541 13194139535634 true] [17592186358536 1110 #uuid "f8f51647-bae7-5532-b804-b3b54fdccc4a" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358537 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535634 true] [17592186358537 1111 #uuid "69d414c6-95a7-5add-97a6-f3ec5d96aa43" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358538 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535634 true] [17592186358538 1103 